is the 2016, enhanced version of the 2015 hit 2D platform game Ori and the Blind Forest. A quick google search will tell you that there is virtually no reason, if reason at all to play the original release instead of the Definitive Edition, as the latter gives you more areas to explore, adds in some interesting mechanics, provides an easier difficulty setting for whom it may concern and offers players a plethora of artwork and extras to look at while keeping everything from the original game. If you happened to snatch the original game before the Definitive Edition came out, you also have access to the Definitive Edition.
Coming into Ori and the Blind Forest: Definitive Edition I expected a challenging game with a beautiful presentation, great mechanics and a wonderful soundtrack. I found the game to be indeed challenging and balanced, one of the most beautiful games I've ever played, with its whimsical, magical scenarios and vivid colors, the mechanics to be addictive and very well executed and the soundtrack, albeit somewhat monotone, a great fit for the on-screen action. Throughout the game you gain ability points that you use on a skill tree in order to upgrade the existing skills and powers and gain acess to new ones. The skill tree itself doesn't hold your attention for too long with unnecessary depth, while the changes made on it have a significant impact on how the game feels - pretty cool, as it adds a different type of strategic thinking to the game without compromising the core experience. The clever mechanics make controlling Ori a pleasure all around. I've never played a platform game with a character that feels so powerful and nimble. Backtracking in this game, when needed, is a little less painful due to how awesome it is to get around with the little guy.
To point out a few negative aspects, which for one I find scant but existent, I wish the storyline was more appealing. I liked the characters and saw potential in the narrative at some points, but ultimately it never quite materialized for me. The way the game is narrated is interesting but juvenile at times, and the story itself never amounts to much. I'm not sure which target audience the developers had in mind, but telling from the story my best guess would be somewhere around the pre-teen console/PC gamers. Following upon that reasoning, with how challenging the game feels, I would like to have gone through more intricate sequences of events in the story. This is to say that the objectives are established by the game as if to keep the story as easy to perceive as possible, dumbing it down a bit in the process. Given how the game is so good on most aspects, I take this as a minor gripe and it didn't detract significantly from my enjoyment of the game.
I managed to beat the game with about 90% completion clocking at about 13 hours, which is about as much time you should expect to sink into this game if you take it slow and explore while going through it. I highly recommend using a good controller to play this game, I used a XBOX360 wired controller and it was awesome. Ori and the Blind Forest: Definitive Edition is an amazing game, a game I would love to see my children play one day. I highly recommend it to all ages. A classic of its time.
8.5/10

